With the **same denominator**, the fraction with the larger numerator is larger: $\tfrac{3}{5} > \tfrac{2}{5}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},x=0.65cm,y=0.65cm]
\foreach \i in {0,...,2} {\fill[blue!25] (\i,1.8) rectangle (\i+1,2.8);}
\draw (0,1.8) rectangle (5,2.8);
\foreach \i in {1,...,4} {\draw (\i,1.8)--(\i,2.8);}
\node[right] at (5.3,2.3) {$\frac35$};
\foreach \i in {0,...,1} {\fill[orange!30] (\i,0) rectangle (\i+1,1);}
\draw (0,0) rectangle (5,1);
\foreach \i in {1,...,4} {\draw (\i,0)--(\i,1);}
\node[right] at (5.3,0.5) {$\frac25$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

With the **same numerator**, the fraction with the smaller denominator is larger: $\tfrac{1}{3} > \tfrac{1}{4}$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},x=0.65cm,y=0.65cm]
\fill[blue!25] (0,1.8) rectangle (2,2.8);
\draw (0,1.8) rectangle (6,2.8);
\foreach \i in {2,4} {\draw (\i,1.8)--(\i,2.8);}
\node[right] at (6.3,2.3) {$\frac13$};
\fill[orange!30] (0,0) rectangle (1.5,1);
\draw (0,0) rectangle (6,1);
\foreach \i in {1.5,3,4.5} {\draw (\i,0)--(\i,1);}
\node[right] at (6.3,0.5) {$\frac14$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

